        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          There is no single best month to seal a driveway. There is a best set of conditions: pavement and air at 50°F or warmer and climbing, a dry surface, low to moderate humidity, and at least a full day of dry weather afterward. Your job is to find the stretch of the calendar where those conditions come together most reliably where you live, and then pick the right morning within it.
        </p>

        <TopPick />

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Short Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            For most of the United States, the most dependable sealing weather runs from late spring through early fall. Within that range, aim for a stretch of two or three dry days with daytime highs comfortably above 50°F, overnight lows that stay above the label minimum, and moderate humidity. Start in mid-morning once the dew is gone, so each coat gets a full afternoon of warmth and sun.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">The Conditions Most Labels Ask For</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">50°F+</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Air and pavement, rising</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">24-48 hr</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Dry forecast after sealing</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">Dry</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Surface before you start</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">Mid-AM</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Best start, after the dew</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Those conditions come from the labels on common water-based asphalt sealers, which typically require air and surface temperatures of at least 50°F during application and for a period after, plus no rain for 24 hours or more. Some products set higher temperature minimums or longer rain-free periods; check yours before planning. The figures above are a baseline, not a guarantee.
          </p>

          <p>
            Two other timing questions come before weather. First, is the driveway old enough? New asphalt needs roughly six to twelve months, or one full warm season, before its first coat; see our guide on <a href="/when-should-you-seal-a-new-asphalt-driveway/">sealing a new asphalt driveway</a>. Second, does it need sealing at all yet? If water still beads on the surface, the last coat is still working, and the right time may be next year.
          </p>

          <p>
            With those answered, the rest of this guide helps you pick a season based on your region, read the forecast for the specific days, and decide what to do if conditions change partway through. If you have not chosen a product, our ranked list of the <a href="/best-asphalt-driveway-sealer-reviews/">best asphalt driveway sealers</a> sorts them by budget and condition.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="season" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Picking the Season: Spring, Summer, or Fall
          </h2>

          <p>
            Late spring is a popular choice because it sets the driveway up for the harsh UV of summer and lets you fill cracks opened by the winter. The downside in many regions is unsettled weather: frequent showers, cool nights, and lingering moisture in the pavement after a wet spring. Wait until nighttime temperatures have reliably climbed and the ground has dried out rather than jumping at the first warm weekend.
          </p>

          <p>
            Summer has the warmest, most reliable drying weather in most of the country, which makes for fast curing. The risks are extreme heat and afternoon thunderstorms. When pavement gets very hot in direct midsummer sun, sealer can skin over before it bonds, and some labels set an upper temperature limit. In hot regions, morning or late-afternoon application often works better than midday in July or August.
          </p>

          <p>
            Early fall is arguably the best season in many climates. Days are still warm, humidity often drops, storms are less frequent, and the fresh coat goes into winter protecting the surface from freeze-thaw water. The risk is waiting too long. Shorter days, cooler nights, and heavier dew late in the fall slow curing, and a coat that never fully cures before freezing weather can fail by spring.
          </p>

