                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Southeast, Gulf Coast</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Spring and fall</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Daily summer storms and humidity</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Desert Southwest</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Spring and fall; early mornings in summer</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Very hot pavement</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Pacific Northwest</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Roughly July to early September</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Fall rain, coastal fog</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="temperature" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Temperature: Air, Pavement, and Overnight
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most water-based asphalt sealers ask for at least 50°F, in both air and pavement temperature, during application and for a period afterward. The phrase that appears on many labels is 50°F and rising, which means starting when the day is warming, not cooling. A 52°F afternoon that drops to 40°F at night is not a good sealing day, even though the temperature was technically above the minimum at the start.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pavement temperature can differ a lot from air temperature. Black asphalt in direct sun can be far hotter than the air on a summer afternoon, while shaded pavement on a cool morning can be colder than the air. An inexpensive infrared thermometer gives a quick reading. On cool days, measure the shadiest part of the driveway, since that is the area most likely to fall short.
          </p>

          <p>
            Overnight lows matter as much as daytime highs. Curing continues for many hours after a coat is surface dry, and cold nights slow or stall it. Look at the forecast low for the night after each coat and confirm it stays at or above the label minimum. If it drops below, the coating may stay soft or fail to form a proper film.
          </p>

          <p>
            At the other extreme, very hot pavement can cause sealer to dry on top too quickly. Some labels set an upper limit; others advise misting the surface lightly with water before application or working in the morning. For more detail on both ends of the range, see our guide on <a href="/what-temperature-to-seal-driveway/">what temperature to seal a driveway</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="moisture" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Rain, Dew, Humidity, and Sprinklers
          </h2>

          <p>
            Sealer needs a dry surface to bond. After rain or washing, allow time for the pavement to dry all the way through, at least 24 hours of dry weather in most cases and longer for shaded or low areas. Press a paper towel into a dark-looking spot; if it picks up moisture, wait. Sealer applied over damp asphalt is a leading cause of peeling within the first year.
          </p>

          <p>
            After application, most labels call for at least 24 hours without rain, and some recommend 48. Read the hourly forecast, not just the daily summary, and look at the chance of storms in the afternoon and overnight. A 30% chance of an evening thunderstorm in summer is a real risk on a freshly sealed driveway. Our guide on <a href="/rain-after-driveway-sealing/">rain after driveway sealing</a> covers what happens if you get caught.
          </p>

          <p>
            Humidity slows evaporation. On a muggy day, sealer can stay tacky far longer than the label suggests, even in warm temperatures. Moderate humidity is fine; very high humidity, fog, or coastal marine air can extend drying significantly. When you have a choice between a hot, humid day and a mild, dry one, the mild, dry day usually produces the better result.
          </p>

          <p>
            Dew is the quiet problem in late summer and fall. Clear nights after warm days produce heavy dew that can leave a cloudy or mottled finish on a coat that has not fully cured. Starting earlier in the day gives the coat more time to set before dew forms. Turn off lawn sprinklers that reach the driveway for a day before and after sealing as well.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="time-of-day" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Time of Day and Planning the Weekend
          </h2>

          <p>
            In most conditions, mid-morning is the best time to start. By then the dew has evaporated, the pavement is warming, and the coat has most of the day to set before evening. Starting at dawn often means sealing over dew-damp pavement; starting after mid-afternoon leaves the coat to face falling temperatures and evening moisture before it has dried.
          </p>

          <p>
            In very hot climates or in midsummer, shift earlier. Start as soon as the surface is dry and finish before the pavement gets extremely hot in the early afternoon. Late-afternoon application can also work in hot regions, provided the overnight low stays well above the label minimum and there is no dew or rain risk.
          </p>

          <p>
            Plan the whole job around the weather window. A good pattern is cleaning and crack filling on day one, sealing on day two, and keeping cars off through day three or four. Check that the forecast holds for all of those days before starting. If you only have a weekend and the forecast is shaky, it is better to postpone than to rush.
          </p>

          <p>
            Weekday mornings can be a smart choice if you can arrange them. Traffic and deliveries are often lighter, neighbors are less likely to be washing cars or running sprinklers, and you avoid weekend demand for sealer and contractors. Our guide on <a href="/how-to-seal-a-driveway/">how to seal a driveway</a> covers how long each stage of the job takes.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="weather-turns" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. What to Do When the Weather Turns
          </h2>

          <p>
            If a storm appears on radar before you start, do not start. Sealer that sits in a bucket keeps; sealer washed into the street does not. If rain shows up while you are partway through, finish the section you are on to a straight line, stop, and cover what you can. Rinse tools before the sealer dries on them. Resume once the surface has dried fully, overlapping the edge only slightly.
          </p>

          <p>
            If rain hits a fresh coat within the first hour or two, damage is likely: streaks, thin areas, and runoff onto adjacent concrete. Rinse any sealer runoff from walkways and the street promptly while it is still wet. Once the driveway dries, sweep off loose material and recoat the damaged areas on the next good day. After several hours of warm weather, light rain usually causes little harm.
          </p>

          <p>
            If temperatures drop unexpectedly overnight, the coat will dry more slowly. Keep cars off longer, check the surface with a fingernail before driving on it, and if it is still soft, give it another warm day. A coat that gets frost on it before curing may need to be redone in spring. Our guide on <a href="/how-long-for-driveway-sealer-to-dry/">driveway sealer dry times</a> covers how to judge readiness.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE POSTPONE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">When the forecast is marginal, wait a week.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A delayed seal costs nothing. A coat applied in borderline weather can peel, stay soft, or wash away, and fixing it means scraping and redoing the work. If temperature, rain, or humidity is close to the label limits, pick a better window instead.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Reading the Air Temperature Only</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The air may be 55°F while shaded pavement is still in the 40s after a cold night. Sealer responds to the surface it is on. Check pavement temperature with an infrared thermometer, especially in shade, and confirm the overnight low stays above the label minimum.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Waiting Until Late Fall</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Late fall days can feel pleasant, but short days, cold nights, and heavy dew slow curing badly. A coat that does not cure before the first freeze can peel by spring. In northern regions, finish by mid-September or so; elsewhere, aim for early fall rather than late.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting the Daily Forecast Icon</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A sun icon for the day says little about a 6 p.m. thunderstorm or overnight showers. Read the hourly forecast for the day of sealing and the following 24 to 48 hours. If the chance of rain is meaningful at any point in that window, wait.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing Right After a Wash or Rain</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The surface may look dry within an hour, but moisture lingers in the pores, cracks, and shaded areas. Sealer laid over that moisture will not bond well. Give the driveway at least a full day of dry weather and check damp-looking spots before starting. Shaded edges often take the longest.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Starting in the Afternoon</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                An afternoon start leaves each coat little time before temperatures drop and dew forms, which slows drying and can cloud the finish. Start mid-morning once the dew is gone. In very hot weather, start early to avoid the hottest pavement temperatures. A morning start also leaves time for the second coat.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What month is best to seal a driveway?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on your region. In northern states, late August and early September are often ideal, with reliable warmth and fewer storms. In the mid-Atlantic and central states, late spring and early fall work well. In the Southeast and Southwest, spring and fall avoid summer storms and extreme heat. In the Pacific Northwest, July through early September is the most dependable window.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it better to seal a driveway in spring or fall?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Both can work. Spring sealing prepares the driveway for summer sun and lets you fix winter cracks, but spring weather is often wet and cool. Early fall usually brings more stable, drier weather and protects the surface heading into winter. The key with fall is not to leave it too late, because cold nights and dew slow curing significantly.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you seal a driveway in summer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Summer offers the warmest, most reliable curing weather in most regions. The main concerns are very hot pavement, which can make sealer dry too quickly on top, and afternoon thunderstorms. In hot climates, start early in the morning or apply in late afternoon if overnight temperatures stay warm, and always check the hourly forecast.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long after rain can I seal my driveway?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Wait for at least a full day of dry weather after rain in most cases, and longer if the driveway is shaded or has low spots that hold water. The surface needs to be dry, not just dry-looking. Press a paper towel into darker areas to check. Sealer applied over trapped moisture often peels within the first year.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I seal my driveway in direct sunlight?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually, yes, and sun helps drying. The exception is extremely hot pavement, where sealer can skin over before it bonds. Some labels set an upper temperature limit or suggest lightly misting the surface first. In hot climates, plan to work in the morning before the pavement reaches peak temperature, or in late afternoon if nights stay warm.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the latest in the year I can seal?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on your climate, but the rule is that both daytime and overnight temperatures must stay at or above the label minimum, usually 50°F, for at least a day or two after sealing. In northern regions that often means finishing by mid-September. In milder regions, October may still work. Once nights are regularly cold, wait until spring.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does time of day matter when sealing a driveway?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Mid-morning, once the dew has burned off, is usually best. It gives each coat most of the day to dry before temperatures drop and evening dew forms. Starting too early risks damp pavement; starting too late leaves the coat to face cooling and moisture before it sets. In very hot weather, shift the start earlier.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

