        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            The best time to seal a driveway is whenever warm, dry, stable weather lines up for two or three days in a row: 50°F or warmer and rising, a dry surface, moderate humidity, and no rain for at least a day afterward. In most regions that means late spring through early fall, with early fall often the most dependable.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Pick your season by region, read the hourly forecast rather than the daily icon, start mid-morning after the dew, and postpone without regret when conditions are marginal. A well-timed coat bonds properly and lasts its full two to three years; a rushed one can need redoing by spring.
          </p>
